# SYNTAX TEST "text.html.tiddlywiki5" "A style block closes only on a closer at the first column"

# styleblock.js ends its body on `^@@` — parseBlocksTerminated skips the indentation first and
# then asks the terminator at that position, where `^` cannot match. So an indented `@@` closes
# nothing: at the head of a block it opens a nested, empty style block of its own, and inside a
# paragraph it stays the paragraph's text. A reader counting closers counts the ones at column 0.

@@color:red;
#<-- punctuation.definition.markup.other.style.styleblock.begin.tiddlywiki5
    @@
#   ^^ - punctuation.definition.markup.other.style.styleblock.end.tiddlywiki5

@@
#<-- punctuation.definition.markup.other.style.styleblock.end.tiddlywiki5
@@
#<-- punctuation.definition.markup.other.style.styleblock.end.tiddlywiki5

# The next block opens where the host opens it, with its style read as a style.
@@color:blue;
#<-- punctuation.definition.markup.other.style.styleblock.begin.tiddlywiki5
# ^^^^^ support.type.property-name.css
y
@@
#<-- punctuation.definition.markup.other.style.styleblock.end.tiddlywiki5

# CONTROL: a closer at the first column closes the block it stands in.
@@color:green;
#<-- punctuation.definition.markup.other.style.styleblock.begin.tiddlywiki5
z
@@
#<-- punctuation.definition.markup.other.style.styleblock.end.tiddlywiki5

After the block.
#<----- - markup.other.style.styleblock.tiddlywiki5

# A closer carrying text after its `@@` hands that text to a paragraph, which runs on to the next
# blank line — so a style line below it reads as the paragraph's text, never as a new block.
@@
#<-- punctuation.definition.markup.other.style.styleblock.begin.tiddlywiki5

@@color:blue;
#<-- punctuation.definition.markup.other.style.styleblock.end.tiddlywiki5
# ^^^^^ meta.paragraph.tiddlywiki5
@@background-color:red;
#<-- - punctuation.definition.markup.other.style.styleblock.begin.tiddlywiki5
#<-- - markup.other.style.styleblock.tiddlywiki5
